#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Fast gate. A commit hook that takes a minute is a commit hook people bypass,
# so this runs the fast subset `mise.toml` calls `pre-commit` and nothing else —
# named there rather than listed here, because a list in a comment is a copy. The
# expensive checks — every target, every feature end, every feature subset, the
# tests, the docs, the browser target — belong to pre-push, which runs far less
# often.
#
# Bypass with `git commit --no-verify`. See CONTRIBUTING.md for when that is
# legitimate; the short version is "the hook itself is broken", never "the
# check is inconvenient".
set -euo pipefail

# Chain to whatever hook was configured before this repo took over
# `core.hooksPath` — a global secret scanner, most often. Setting a repo-local
# hooks path silently REPLACES a global one, so without this the repo would
# quietly disable a guard the developer set up for every repository they own.
# `scripts/install-hooks.sh` records the previous path here.
chain="$(git config --get hooks.chain || true)"
if [ -n "$chain" ] && [ -x "$chain/pre-commit" ]; then
    "$chain/pre-commit" "$@"
fi

# Which commit is the one being built ON? An ordinary commit extends `HEAD`, so
# comparing the index against `HEAD` describes it exactly. An `--amend` REPLACES
# `HEAD`, so the commit it produces is built on `HEAD`'s parent — and comparing
# against `HEAD` then describes the wrong thing.
#
# That is the defect this exists for, and it is narrower than "amends are not
# gated". An amend that only reworded a message diffs empty against `HEAD` and
# skips, which is harmless when `HEAD` itself was gated. What it cannot do is
# **repair a commit that was never gated** — one made with `--no-verify`, or by
# this very early exit under an older version of this hook. That amend looks to
# the developer like a second chance and is not one: the gate does not run, and
# nothing says so.
#
# Consequence worth stating, because it is a real cost and not an oversight: a
# message-only amend of a Rust-touching commit now DOES re-run the gate. Git does
# not record whether a commit was verified, so "was `HEAD` already gated?" is not
# a question this hook can ask — only "does the commit I am about to write touch
# code the gate cares about?". Re-running is the answer that cannot be wrong in
# the dangerous direction. It is cheap on a warm tree, which an amend always has,
# because the commit it is amending just built one.
#
# NO SECOND-COUNT IS QUOTED HERE ANY MORE, ON PURPOSE, and the reason is worth more
# than the number was. This comment used to claim `~0.5 s`, measured when `mise run
# pre-commit` was three tasks. It is five now, and the two additions are both
# non-trivial Python, so the old figure had stopped being merely stale.
#
# The first attempt to replace it was withdrawn, which is the useful part: the same
# three non-cargo tasks timed **0.04 s and 0.27 s for `framing`** across two
# sessions minutes apart, and `anchors-selftest` ranged 0.21–0.44 s — a spread
# driven by filesystem warmth, by whether `/usr/bin/time` forks a shell, and by
# another compiler running in a sibling worktree. **The variance was larger than the
# quantity being claimed**, so any single number written here is a coin flip
# presented as evidence.
#
# So this comment states the shape and owns no figure: the hook is cheap on a warm
# tree, and an amend always has one. A wall-clock figure needs a stated method and a
# quiet machine, and it belongs wherever that measurement is recorded — not inline,
# where it rots invisibly and is read as fact.
#
# There is NO signal for an amend in git's environment, and this was verified
# rather than assumed: every `GIT_*` variable is byte-identical between an
# ordinary commit and an amend, and `ORIG_HEAD` is unset for both — `commit
# --amend` does not write it. The parent process is the only thing that knows,
# and the hook's parent IS `git commit`.
#
# The check over-fires on `git commit -m '... --amend ...'`, because the message
# is in that command line too. That costs one extra gate run and no correctness,
# which is the direction to be wrong in.
#
# ── AND A REBASE IS REPLAYING, NOT AUTHORING, which is the one place the
# ── paragraph above must not apply.
#
# A plain replay never reaches this hook, so the case that matters is the one that
# does. MEASURED on git 2.54.0 (Apple Git-157), inside a linked worktree:
#
#     git rebase <upstream>, two commits replayed   hook fires 0 times
#     git rebase -i with a `reword` step            hook fires ONCE
#     the `edit` stop itself                        hook fires 0 times
#     git rebase --continue after that stop         hook fires 0 times
#
# A `reword` fires because git's sequencer spawns `git commit --amend
# --no-gpg-sign -e --allow-empty` and does NOT pass `--no-verify`. The check above
# then sees `--amend` and is right to — it IS an amend — so the gate runs against
# a commit the developer is replaying rather than writing.
#
# That is worth refusing for a reason stronger than its cost. `lint-fast` runs
# clippy against the WORKING TREE, and mid-rebase the working tree sits at an
# intermediate commit, which has no obligation to be clippy-clean. A failure there
# stops the rebase mid-flight, and the only way out is `git commit --amend
# --no-verify` and then `git rebase --continue` — which trains exactly the bypass
# this file's header calls illegitimate. A gate has to fail where the developer
# can act on it, and inside a replay they cannot: repairing that tree is a
# different operation from the reword they asked for.
#
# The incoherence is what settles it. Within ONE `git rebase -i`, a `pick` of an
# ungated commit skips and a `reword` of that same commit gates. The discriminator
# is whether a message happened to change, which is orthogonal to whether the code
# was ever checked. Skipping here widens nothing that a plain replay does not
# already leave open; it makes the two agree.
#
# WHAT IS SKIPPED IS THE DETECTOR AND NOT THE HOOK, and that difference is the
# whole design. Clearing `amend_base` sends a rebase-driven commit down the
# ordinary path, which is already right for both shapes it meets there:
#
#   - at a `reword` the index matches HEAD, so the diff is EMPTY and the early
#     exit below fires. Measured: `git diff --cached --name-only` prints nothing.
#   - at an `edit` stop where the developer STAGED code, that diff is NOT empty,
#     so the gate still runs. That is the one moment inside a rebase when a human
#     is genuinely authoring, and it stays gated.
#
# An early `exit 0` here would have thrown the second one away.
#
# `git rev-parse --git-path` rather than a literal `.git/rebase-merge`, and that
# is not a style preference. In a LINKED WORKTREE `.git` is a FILE, so
# `[ -d .git/rebase-merge ]` is false even in the middle of a rebase — measured
# `no` during an actual `reword`. This repo is developed in several worktrees at
# once, so the literal form would be dead code exactly where the work happens.
rebasing=no
if [ -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge)" ] ||
    [ -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-apply)" ]; then
    rebasing=yes
fi

amend_base=""
if [ "$rebasing" = no ] && ps -o args= -p "$PPID" 2>/dev/null | grep -qw -- '--amend'; then
    # The empty tree when amending a root commit, so there is one code path here
    # rather than a special case that only fires on a repository's first commit.
    amend_base="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD~1 || git hash-object -t tree /dev/null)"
fi

# Nothing in the resulting commit that we lint? Then there is nothing for this
# hook to say. Two spellings rather than an array, because `set -u` plus an empty
# `"${a[@]}"` is an error on the bash 3.2 that macOS still ships as /bin/bash.
if [ -n "$amend_base" ]; then
    linted="$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR "$amend_base")"
else
    linted="$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR)"
fi
# ── A HERE-STRING AND NOT A PIPE, which is the difference between a gate and a
# ── SILENT BYPASS. This was `printf '%s\n' "$linted" | grep -qE ...`.
#
# `grep -q` exits 0 at its FIRST match and closes the pipe. On a large staged list
# `printf` is then killed by SIGPIPE and exits 141, `pipefail` makes the whole
# pipeline non-zero, `!` inverts that to true, and the hook takes the `exit 0`
# below — **skipping the gate on a commit that stages Rust**, with a zero exit
# status and nothing printed. The failure runs the wrong way round: the larger the
# commit, the likelier it is ungated, so a big refactor or a vendored import is
# exactly what goes unchecked.
#
# MEASURED, control first, in throwaway repositories with a stub `mise` that
# reports whether the gate was reached at all. `a.rs` sorts early, so the match
# lands near the top while the rest of the list is still unwritten:
#
#     staged path list    with the pipe    with this here-string
#     65,001 bytes        gate RAN         gate RAN
#     65,566 bytes        gate SKIPPED     gate RAN
#     226,026 bytes       gate SKIPPED     gate RAN
#     2,825,026 bytes     gate SKIPPED     gate RAN
#
# **`git commit` exited 0 in every one of those runs**, which is why nobody would
# ever have noticed. The flip brackets 65,536 bytes — one 64 KiB pipe buffer — so
# it is a kernel constant rather than a figure of this repository's, and a machine
# with a different capacity flips somewhere else. What is invariant is the shape:
# a match near the START of a list bigger than one buffer kills the writer.
#
# **THE TRAP, MEASURED THE HARD WAY, for whoever replays this.** The first harness
# asked `printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q 'GATE RAN'` of the commit's own output — and
# git prints one `create mode` line per staged file, so that pipeline reproduced
# the defect under test INSIDE the measurement and reported SKIP for both
# spellings alike. The first set of figures written here came from it and was
# wrong in the direction that flatters the fix. Redirect to a file and let grep
# read the file: never measure a pipe bug through a pipe.
#
# `<<<` hands grep a seekable temp file, which has no writer to kill and no
# pipeline for `pipefail` to judge. Byte-for-byte the same input: `<<<` appends
# exactly one LF, which is what `printf '%s\n'` did.
if ! grep -qE '\.rs$|Cargo\.toml$|Cargo\.lock$|rustfmt\.toml$' <<<"$linted"; then
    exit 0
fi

cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

if command -v mise >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    exec mise run pre-commit
fi

# `mise` is how the gate is defined, and this path runs the same checks so that a
# contributor without it is still gated rather than silently ungated.
#
# It said "the same two commands" while `pre-commit` already ran THREE tasks —
# `framing` was never inlined — then `anchors` made it four and `anchors-selftest`
# five. **No enumeration of them is written in this comment any more: the lines
# below ARE the list, in `mise.toml`'s order, and a second copy of it up here is
# one more thing to go stale.**
#
# This sentence went on to say `task-lists` made it six, and that was left standing
# after `task-lists` moved to `gate` alone — so ONE FILE stated the length of this
# list twice, as five above and as six here. `task-lists` cannot catch it: the check
# reads only the region below the `note: 'mise' is not installed` anchor, and both
# sentences sit above it. That is the argument for naming no count at all.
#
# The script tasks are invoked rather than reimplemented; a second copy of their
# logic is a second thing to drift. Each reads its own root and runs standalone
# without `mise` in the environment.
#
# THIS LIST IS A COPY AND `mise.toml` IS THE AUTHORITY. It has been one task short
# twice, and the sentence that used to close this paragraph — "nothing enforces
# that" — is the reason `.mise/tasks/task-lists` exists. It reads the manifest's
# `pre-commit` list and asserts that every task in it is invoked below, in the
# manifest's order, with nothing extra beside it.
#
# **Where that bites is the PUSH and never the commit, and the reason is simpler
# than the one this paragraph used to give.** `task-lists` is not in the
# `pre-commit` list at all — `mise.toml` puts it in `gate` alone and says so — so it
# does not run at a commit for the plain reason that a commit does not run it.
#
# The reason written here before was that the filter above only reaches the gate
# when the commit stages Rust or a cargo manifest, `mise.toml` being neither. That
# sentence described `task-lists` as running inside `pre-commit`, which it stopped
# doing when it moved. **Both readings reach the same conclusion, which is exactly
# why the wrong one survived** — and a contributor acting on it would widen the
# filter and change nothing, because widening a list `task-lists` is not in cannot
# add it. So a task added to the manifest and forgotten below survives the commit
# that adds it, and fails `mise run gate` at the push.
#
# Worth naming for what it was: a copy of the `pre-commit` list's behaviour that
# disagreed with the manifest, inside the hook whose drift this file's own guard
# exists to stop. `task-lists` compares the INVOCATIONS below against the manifest
# and reads none of this prose, so nothing but a reader was ever going to catch it.
#
# Not resolved the way `pre-push` resolves it, and the difference is deliberate.
# That hook now REFUSES without `mise`, because it claims to be the whole gate and
# CI is off by decision, so a subset there is a false green. This hook claims the
# opposite: its header says it runs the fast subset and nothing else, and that the
# expensive checks belong to `pre-push`. A
# hook that is avowedly a subset cannot be made a false green by being a subset —
# it can only be made one by covering less than the list it names, which is what
# this fixes.
echo "note: 'mise' is not installed - running the same checks directly" >&2
.mise/tasks/framing
.mise/tasks/anchors
.mise/tasks/anchors-selftest
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo clippy -p keylink --lib --all-features -- -D warnings
